Source: https://github.com/L337-org/quaffed
Comment: usr/bin/quaff is a statically linked binary, so it contains every
 crate it is built from as well as quaffed itself.  quaffed is the first
 paragraph below; each paragraph after it is a part of the binary that came
 from elsewhere, with that part's own copyright holders and licence.
 .
 The binary is built from crates rather than from files in quaffed's source,
 so a paragraph for a crate names it as crates.io/NAME/* and one for a part of
 the Rust toolchain as toolchain/NAME/*.  The crates include procedural macros
 and the crates those are built from, because the code a macro generates is
 compiled into the binary.  Where a crate is offered under a choice of
 licences, its paragraph says which one it is used under here.
 .
 The binary also contains LLVM's libunwind, from the Rust toolchain, whose
 licence's LLVM exception waives the notice for code embedded in a binary.
 .
 The crates are checked against the binary's dependency graph by
 crates/quaffed/tests/packaging.rs in the source repository.  The toolchain's
 parts are outside that graph, so they are kept here by hand.

